# Generated by Claude
"""The window.

Header, then the work area, then a log, then the buttons. The work area is a
stack of two pages, because a repository with nothing to commit is a
different screen and not an empty version of the same one: a tree with a
header row and no rows under it, next to a panel saying "select a file on the
left", reads as a program that failed to load rather than as good news.

The rules the UI enforces, all of them there to make a wrong commit hard:

* A group whose message is still just its prefix cannot be committed. The
  buttons stay *enabled* and say why when clicked -- an earlier version
  disabled them, and a dead button with the reason only in the status bar
  reads as a broken program rather than as a question waiting for an answer.
  Clicking now jumps to the box that needs typing and outlines it in amber.
* Unticking a file rewrites its group's generated message, so the subject
  can never name a post the commit does not contain -- unless the user has
  typed over it, in which case their text is never touched.
* Push is confirmed separately from commit, because a push to master starts
  the Pages deploy.
* Merge in progress, conflicted files or a detached HEAD show a banner and
  block committing entirely. That one *does* disable the buttons: it is not
  a question the user can answer from this window.

Every background result carries the scan generation it belongs to. A refresh
calls `tree.clear()`, which deletes the item objects; a worker that finishes
afterwards would otherwise write into freed widgets.

The interface is English, matching `_config.yml`'s `lang: en`, the `config:
switch UI to English` commit in this repo's history, and the sibling tools.
"""
